    "command": "bun \"$TMPDIR/adjudicate-cozrgxsb/repro.ts\"",
    "output": "Fixture changes example.ts to contain: export const magic = \"%PDF-\";\nGit numstat: 1\\t1\\texample.ts\nHEAD: binaryPaths=[\"example.ts\"], headTextPaths=[], audit=[{\"rule\":\"binary-content\",\"severity\":\"block\",\"file\":\"example.ts\"}]\nBASE: identical classification and blocking finding."
   },
   "evidence": "HEAD line 834 uses `binary: bytes.includes(0) || bytes.includes(Buffer.from(\"%PDF-\"))`; line 841 removes such paths from the text set. This identical code exists at base. The reproduction contains no NUL, and its complete text remains visible in the patch, contradicting the audit's claim that binary bytes hide its content."
  },
  "B": {
   "verdict": "REAL",
   "failure_scenario": "Someone edits a source or test file that mentions the PDF signature text \"%PDF-\". The audit blocks the change as binary and says it contains \"binary bytes, such as a NUL\", but the file has no NUL, so the author can go looking for bytes that are not there.",
   "reproduction": {
    "command": "Clone the checkout into $TMPDIR, check out head, then: bun run.ts <clone> a682ada7 (a script that calls diffSince and prints binaryPaths). Also count NUL bytes and '%PDF-' in both changed files with python.",
    "output": "{\"binaryPaths\":[\"src/git/repos.ts\",\"test/git-integrity.test.ts\"]}; repos.ts: 0 NULs, 1 '%PDF-'; git-integrity.test.ts: 0 NULs, 3 '%PDF-'"
   },
   "evidence": "src/git/repos.ts:834 `binary: bytes.includes(0) || bytes.includes(Buffer.from(\"%PDF-\"))` matches the signature anywhere in the blob, and line 838 then drops the path from `raw`. The `!afterText.raw.has(path) && !inert.has(path)` branch at lines 961-963 puts it in binaryPaths. The message at src/gates/audit.ts:286 says \"binary bytes, such as a NUL\". The anywhere-match is deliberate: the test 'whole-blob classification\u2026' expects late-signature.txt to be flagged. A text file that only mentions the signature is still a false positive. This change does not touch these lines."
  }
 },
